# Secret. Copy to ${SF_SECRETS_DIR}/migrate.env (outside the repository, 0600). Read by: migrate and migrate-safe
# (the one-shot migration jobs), backup and restore-check (they must read every table). It is the OWNER role
# force_migrator, created by db-roles.sql: it owns every schema object and is used by short-lived jobs only,
# never by a long-running service. The API and the worker have their own roles (api.env, worker.env).
# Staging credentials only: never reuse production credentials (OPS-1). Never the PostgreSQL superuser.
#
# Default initial staging (self-hosted-db profile): host `postgres` on the private backend network, e.g.
#   postgres://force_migrator:PASSWORD@postgres:5432/DB
# The password is the one set for force_migrator by the owner's db-roles run (db-admin.env). Percent-encode
# reserved characters in the password (a hex password needs none: openssl rand -hex 32).
#
# Accepted URL shape (the ops scripts refuse anything else): postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST[:PORT]/DBNAME with
# DBNAME made of A-Z a-z 0-9 _ . - only, a bracketed IPv6 host ([::1]:5432), and a query that may carry ONLY
# sslmode, sslrootcert, connect_timeout (no host=, port=, dbname=, password=, options=, no "%" in the query).
#
# Managed PostgreSQL >= 16 (target, V-04/V-15): private networking; TLS verified:
DATABASE_URL=postgres://force_migrator:REPLACE_PASSWORD@REPLACE_HOST:5432/REPLACE_DB?sslmode=verify-full
#
# sslmode=verify-full needs the provider's CA certificate. node-postgres and libpq do NOT use the system store
# automatically for a private provider CA: supply it as a file and name it in the URL, for example
#   ...?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/run/secrets/db-ca.pem
# (placeholder path: use your own). The file is not a secret but is installation-specific: keep it next to the
# env files under ${SF_SECRETS_DIR}, mount it READ-ONLY at that same path in EVERY container that has a
# DATABASE_URL: migrate, migrate-safe, api, worker, backup and restore-check (a compose override file with one
# `volumes:` entry per service). The same applies to RESTORE_TEST_DATABASE_URL in restore-test.env. How each
# provider publishes its CA is NEEDS VALIDATION at the managed-database rollout (V-04); never disable
# verification (sslmode=require without verification, or rejectUnauthorized=false) to get past a certificate error.
